<p style="text-align: left;">Data set A and data set B each consist of <math alttext="27"><mn>27</mn>
</math> values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"550cc1d4-b5e4-4345-a166-fa317e11b3e6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is less than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"75c98841-2439-4655-894c-cd5c15f8fce6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is equal to the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"dd1445f9-7778-4f9b-8409-e731b2055dea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There is not enough information to compare the means of the data sets.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is correct. The mean value of a data set is the sum of the values of the data set divided by the number of values in the data set. When a data set is represented in a frequency table, the sum of the values in the data set is the sum of the products of each value and its frequency. For data set A, the sum of products of each value and its frequency is <math alttext="30 left parenthesis 2 right parenthesis plus 34 left parenthesis 4 right parenthesis plus 38 left parenthesis 5 right parenthesis plus 42 left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis plus 46 left parenthesis 9 right parenthesis"><mn>30</mn><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>34</mn><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>38</mn><mfenced><mn>5</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>42</mn><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>46</mn><mfenced><mn>9</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="1,094"><mn>1,094</mn></math>. It's given that there are <math alttext="27"><mn>27</mn>
</math> values in data set A. Therefore, the mean of data set A is <math alttext="StartFraction 1,094 Over 27 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>1,094</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext="40.52"><mn>40.52</mn></math>. Similarly, the mean of data B is <math alttext="StartFraction 958 Over 27 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>958</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext="35.48"><mn>35.48</mn></math>. Therefore, the mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>
<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$manifold$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c178d4da","external_id":"45c46b40-e7e6-4f3e-bbb1-22e0862c3911","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":"3f4f7a39-0572-43fd-83ed-b10337978258","uId":"2d9a050c-7de8-47ac-8e5a-675fc0e70a30","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.C.","skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<figure class=\"table\"><figure class=\"table\"><figure class=\"table\"><table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Value</th>\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Data set A frequency</th>\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Data set B frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"34\"><mn>34</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"38\"><mn>38</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"42\"><mn>42</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"46\"><mn>46</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure></figure></figure>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Data set A and data set B each consist of <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math> values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"550cc1d4-b5e4-4345-a166-fa317e11b3e6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is less than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"75c98841-2439-4655-894c-cd5c15f8fce6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is equal to the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"dd1445f9-7778-4f9b-8409-e731b2055dea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There is not enough information to compare the means of the data sets.</p>"}],"keys":["a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. The mean value of a data set is the sum of the values of the data set divided by the number of values in the data set. When a data set is represented in a frequency table, the sum of the values in the data set is the sum of the products of each value and its frequency. For data set A, the sum of products of each value and its frequency is <math alttext=\"30 left parenthesis 2 right parenthesis plus 34 left parenthesis 4 right parenthesis plus 38 left parenthesis 5 right parenthesis plus 42 left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis plus 46 left parenthesis 9 right parenthesis\"><mn>30</mn><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>34</mn><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>38</mn><mfenced><mn>5</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>42</mn><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>46</mn><mfenced><mn>9</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"1,094\"><mn>1,094</mn></math>. It's given that there are <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math> values in data set A. Therefore, the mean of data set A is <math alttext=\"StartFraction 1,094 Over 27 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1,094</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext=\"40.52\"><mn>40.52</mn></math>. Similarly, the mean of data B is <math alttext=\"StartFraction 958 Over 27 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>958</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext=\"35.48\"><mn>35.48</mn></math>. Therefore, the mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":"manifold","position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1691007959833,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"c178d4da","skill_cd":"Q.C.","score_band_range_cd":6,"skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","createDate":1691007959833,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":null,"external_id":"45c46b40-e7e6-4f3e-bbb1-22e0862c3911","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"2d9a050c-7de8-47ac-8e5a-675fc0e70a30","difficulty":"H"},"raw_detail":{"keys":["a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is correct. The mean value of a data set is the sum of the values of the data set divided by the number of values in the data set. When a data set is represented in a frequency table, the sum of the values in the data set is the sum of the products of each value and its frequency. For data set A, the sum of products of each value and its frequency is <math alttext=\"30 left parenthesis 2 right parenthesis plus 34 left parenthesis 4 right parenthesis plus 38 left parenthesis 5 right parenthesis plus 42 left parenthesis 7 right parenthesis plus 46 left parenthesis 9 right parenthesis\"><mn>30</mn><mfenced><mn>2</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>34</mn><mfenced><mn>4</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>38</mn><mfenced><mn>5</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>42</mn><mfenced><mn>7</mn></mfenced><mo>+</mo><mn>46</mn><mfenced><mn>9</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"1,094\"><mn>1,094</mn></math>. It's given that there are <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math> values in data set A. Therefore, the mean of data set A is <math alttext=\"StartFraction 1,094 Over 27 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1,094</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext=\"40.52\"><mn>40.52</mn></math>. Similarly, the mean of data B is <math alttext=\"StartFraction 958 Over 27 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>958</mn><mn>27</mn></mfrac></math>, or approximately <math alttext=\"35.48\"><mn>35.48</mn></math>. Therefore, the mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>\n<p>Choice B is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice C is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>\n<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>","origin":"manifold","stem":"<figure class=\"table\"><figure class=\"table\"><figure class=\"table\"><table border=\"1\">\n<tbody>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Value</th>\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Data set A frequency</th>\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"col\">Data set B frequency</th>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"30\"><mn>30</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"34\"><mn>34</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"38\"><mn>38</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"5\"><mn>5</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"42\"><mn>42</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"7\"><mn>7</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"4\"><mn>4</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n<tr style=\"height: 22.3958px;\">\n<th style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\" scope=\"row\"><math alttext=\"46\"><mn>46</mn>\n</math></th>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math></td>\n<td style=\"width: 31.0368%; height: 22.3958px; text-align: center;\"><math alttext=\"2\"><mn>2</mn>\n</math></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></figure></figure></figure>\n<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Data set A and data set B each consist of <math alttext=\"27\"><mn>27</mn>\n</math> values. The table shows the frequencies of the values for each data set. Which of the following statements best compares the means of the two data sets?</p>","externalid":"45c46b40-e7e6-4f3e-bbb1-22e0862c3911","templateid":"bdb03d92-817e-428a-ae07-6e78b18b7172","vaultid":"3f4f7a39-0572-43fd-83ed-b10337978258","type":"mcq","answerOptions":[{"id":"a19f1fec-7115-4e01-9052-fdd03f1b0862","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is greater than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"550cc1d4-b5e4-4345-a166-fa317e11b3e6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is less than the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"75c98841-2439-4655-894c-cd5c15f8fce6","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The mean of data set A is equal to the mean of data set B.</p>"},{"id":"dd1445f9-7778-4f9b-8409-e731b2055dea","content":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">There is not enough information to compare the means of the data sets.</p>"}],"correct_answer":["A"]},"createDate":1691007959833,"updateDate":1691007959833}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959833, 1691007959833),
    ($SATQ$c256b723$SATQ$, $SATQ$967c754c-6ed6-4648-9b7b-4a17946cf25f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.B.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Percentages$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The amount of Hanna's bill for a food order was <math alttext="dollar sign 50"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math>. Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext="20 percent sign"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill. What is the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["10"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["10"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the amount of Hanna's food order was&nbsp;<math alttext="dollar sign 50"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and that Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext="20 percent sign"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill.&nbsp;<math alttext="20 percent sign"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of <math alttext="50"><mn>50</mn>
</math> can be calculated as <math alttext="left parenthesis StartFraction 20 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis left parenthesis 50 right parenthesis"><mfenced><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfenced><mn>50</mn></mfenced></math>, which yields <math alttext="StartFraction 1000 Over 100 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>1000</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>. Therefore, the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave is <math alttext="10"><mn>10</mn>
</math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c256b723","external_id":"967c754c-6ed6-4648-9b7b-4a17946cf25f","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"a61d57c5-d37d-48c0-8b85-45d986703bcd","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.B.","skill_desc":"Percentages","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The amount of Hanna's bill for a food order was <math alttext=\"dollar sign 50\"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math>. Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill. What is the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["10"],"correct_answer":["10"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the amount of Hanna's food order was&nbsp;<math alttext=\"dollar sign 50\"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and that Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill.&nbsp;<math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math> can be calculated as <math alttext=\"left parenthesis StartFraction 20 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis left parenthesis 50 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfenced><mn>50</mn></mfenced></math>, which yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 1000 Over 100 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1000</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave is <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357402,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":"c256b723","skill_cd":"Q.B.","score_band_range_cd":4,"skill_desc":"Percentages","createDate":1730147357402,"program":"SAT","primary_class_cd_desc":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","ibn":"","external_id":"967c754c-6ed6-4648-9b7b-4a17946cf25f","primary_class_cd":"Q","uId":"a61d57c5-d37d-48c0-8b85-45d986703bcd","difficulty":"M"},"raw_detail":{"answerOptions":[],"externalid":"967c754c-6ed6-4648-9b7b-4a17946cf25f","keys":["10"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the amount of Hanna's food order was&nbsp;<math alttext=\"dollar sign 50\"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math> and that Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill.&nbsp;<math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of <math alttext=\"50\"><mn>50</mn>\n</math> can be calculated as <math alttext=\"left parenthesis StartFraction 20 Over 100 EndFraction right parenthesis left parenthesis 50 right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mfrac><mn>20</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></mfenced><mfenced><mn>50</mn></mfenced></math>, which yields <math alttext=\"StartFraction 1000 Over 100 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>1000</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac></math>, or <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>. Therefore, the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave is <math alttext=\"10\"><mn>10</mn>\n</math>.</p>","stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The amount of Hanna's bill for a food order was <math alttext=\"dollar sign 50\"><mo>$</mo><mn>50</mn></math>. Hanna gave a tip of <math alttext=\"20 percent sign\"><mn>20</mn><mo>%</mo></math> of the amount of the bill. What is the amount, in dollars, of the tip Hanna gave?</p>","type":"spr","correct_answer":["10"]},"createDate":1730147357402,"updateDate":1730147357402}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147357402, 1730147357402),
    ($SATQ$c2e7fa6d$SATQ$, $SATQ$a9ed5978-3ec1-4d21-966b-82a5c7c417f3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.A.$SATQ$, $SATQ$Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$spr$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">For an electric field passing through a flat surface perpendicular to it, the electric flux of the electric field through the surface is the product of the electric field&rsquo;s strength and the area of the surface. A certain flat surface consists of two adjacent squares, where the side length, in meters, of the larger square is <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math> times the side length, in meters, of the smaller square. An electric field with strength <math alttext="29.00"><mrow><mn>29.00</mn></mrow></math> volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface, which is perpendicular to the electric field. If the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is <math alttext="4,640 volts dot meters"><mrow><mn>4,640</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, what is the electric flux, in <math alttext="volts dot meters"><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, of the electric field through the larger square?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4176"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4176"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">The correct answer is <math alttext="4,176"><mn>4,176</mn>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the side length of the larger square is <math alttext="3"><mn>3</mn>
</math> times the side length of the smaller square. This means that the area of the larger square is <math alttext="3 squared"><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext="9"><mn>9</mn>
</math>, times the area of the smaller square. If the area of the smaller square is represented by <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math>, then the area of the larger square can be represented by <math alttext="9 x"><mrow>
	<mn>9</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. Therefore, the flat surface of the two adjacent squares has a total area of <math alttext="x plus 9 x"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext="10 x"><mrow>
	<mn>10</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math>. It&rsquo;s given that an electric field with strength <math alttext="29.00"><mn>29.00</mn></math> volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface and the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is <math alttext="4,640 volts dot meters"><mn>4,640</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>. Since it's given that the electric flux is the product of the electric field&rsquo;s strength and the area of the surface, the equation <math alttext="29.00 left parenthesis 10 x right parenthesis equals 4,640"><mn>29.00</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>4,640</mn></math>, or <math alttext="290 x equals 4,640"><mn>290</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,640</mn></math>, can be used to represent this situation. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext="290"><mn>290</mn>
</math> yields <math alttext="x equals 16"><mrow>
	<mi>x</mi>
	<mo>=</mo>
	<mn>16</mn>
</mrow>
</math>. Substituting <math alttext="16"><mn>16</mn>
</math> for <math alttext="x"><mi>x</mi>
</math> in the expression for the area of the larger square, <math alttext="9 x"><mrow>
	<mn>9</mn>
	<mi>x</mi>
</mrow>
</math>, yields <math alttext="9 left parenthesis 16 right parenthesis"><mn>9</mn><mfenced><mn>16</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext="144"><mn>144</mn>
</math>, square meters. Since the area of the larger square is <math alttext="144"><mn>144</mn>
</math> square meters, the electric flux, in <math alttext="volts dot meters"><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, of the electric field through the larger square can be determined by multiplying the area of the larger square by the strength of the electric field. Thus, the electric flux is <math alttext="left parenthesis 144 square meters right parenthesis left parenthesis StartFraction 29.00 volts Over meter EndFraction right parenthesis"><mfenced><mrow><mn>144</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>square</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>29.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext></mrow><mtext>meter</mtext></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext="4,176 volts dot meters"><mn>4,176</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c2e7fa6d","external_id":"a9ed5978-3ec1-4d21-966b-82a5c7c417f3","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"c1d41164-dd36-42e7-8ef2-8b8eca045fcb","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.A.","skill_desc":"Ratios, rates, proportional relationships, and units","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"spr","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">For an electric field passing through a flat surface perpendicular to it, the electric flux of the electric field through the surface is the product of the electric field&rsquo;s strength and the area of the surface. A certain flat surface consists of two adjacent squares, where the side length, in meters, of the larger square is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math> times the side length, in meters, of the smaller square. An electric field with strength <math alttext=\"29.00\"><mrow><mn>29.00</mn></mrow></math> volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface, which is perpendicular to the electric field. If the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is <math alttext=\"4,640 volts dot meters\"><mrow><mn>4,640</mn></mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, what is the electric flux, in <math alttext=\"volts dot meters\"><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, of the electric field through the larger square?</p>","answerOptions":[],"keys":["4176"],"correct_answer":["4176"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math alttext=\"4,176\"><mn>4,176</mn>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that the side length of the larger square is <math alttext=\"3\"><mn>3</mn>\n</math> times the side length of the smaller square. This means that the area of the larger square is <math alttext=\"3 squared\"><msup><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math alttext=\"9\"><mn>9</mn>\n</math>, times the area of the smaller square. If the area of the smaller square is represented by <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math>, then the area of the larger square can be represented by <math alttext=\"9 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Therefore, the flat surface of the two adjacent squares has a total area of <math alttext=\"x plus 9 x\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>9</mn><mi>x</mi></math>, or <math alttext=\"10 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>10</mn>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>. It&rsquo;s given that an electric field with strength <math alttext=\"29.00\"><mn>29.00</mn></math> volts per meter passes uniformly through this surface and the total electric flux of the electric field through this surface is <math alttext=\"4,640 volts dot meters\"><mn>4,640</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>. Since it's given that the electric flux is the product of the electric field&rsquo;s strength and the area of the surface, the equation <math alttext=\"29.00 left parenthesis 10 x right parenthesis equals 4,640\"><mn>29.00</mn><mfenced><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mn>4,640</mn></math>, or <math alttext=\"290 x equals 4,640\"><mn>290</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4,640</mn></math>, can be used to represent this situation. Dividing each side of this equation by <math alttext=\"290\"><mn>290</mn>\n</math> yields <math alttext=\"x equals 16\"><mrow>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n\t<mo>=</mo>\n\t<mn>16</mn>\n</mrow>\n</math>. Substituting <math alttext=\"16\"><mn>16</mn>\n</math> for <math alttext=\"x\"><mi>x</mi>\n</math> in the expression for the area of the larger square, <math alttext=\"9 x\"><mrow>\n\t<mn>9</mn>\n\t<mi>x</mi>\n</mrow>\n</math>, yields <math alttext=\"9 left parenthesis 16 right parenthesis\"><mn>9</mn><mfenced><mn>16</mn></mfenced></math>, or <math alttext=\"144\"><mn>144</mn>\n</math>, square meters. Since the area of the larger square is <math alttext=\"144\"><mn>144</mn>\n</math> square meters, the electric flux, in <math alttext=\"volts dot meters\"><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>, of the electric field through the larger square can be determined by multiplying the area of the larger square by the strength of the electric field. Thus, the electric flux is <math alttext=\"left parenthesis 144 square meters right parenthesis left parenthesis StartFraction 29.00 volts Over meter EndFraction right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>144</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>square</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></mrow></mfenced><mfenced><mfrac><mrow><mn>29.00</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext></mrow><mtext>meter</mtext></mfrac></mfenced></math>, or&nbsp;<math alttext=\"4,176 volts dot meters\"><mn>4,176</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>volts</mtext><mo>&#183;</mo><mtext>meters</mtext></math>.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"image_count":0,"raw_list":{"updateDate":1730147357401,"pPcc":"SAT#Q","questionId":