</math> coaches at the track meet, which of the following expressions represents the number of athletes at the track meet?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"80a1a9ef-5895-40b4-971d-09f45026441b","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction x Over 26 EndFraction\"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>26</mn></mfrac></math></p>"},{"id":"481fa189-401a-4036-80cc-0f98c46f7157","content":"<p><math alttext=\"26 x\"><mn>26</mn><mi>x</mi></math></p>"},{"id":"41256bba-0117-49fc-855f-a4359af789a8","content":"<p><math alttext=\"x plus 26\"><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>26</mn></math></p>"},{"id":"b4be94a6-739f-452e-97ab-9624b1fad8f4","content":"<p><math alttext=\"StartFraction 26 Over x EndFraction\"><mfrac><mn>26</mn><mi>x</mi></mfrac></math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["481fa189-401a-4036-80cc-0f98c46f7157"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. It&rsquo;s given that at a particular track meet, the ratio of coaches to athletes is <math alttext="1"><mn>1</mn>
</math> to <math alttext="26"><mn>26</mn>
