kilometers right parenthesis\"><mfenced><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>nautical</mtext><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>mile</mtext><mo>=</mo><mn>1.852</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mtext>kilometers</mtext></mrow></mfenced></math></p>","keys":["157.8","789/5"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">The correct answer is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"157.8\"><mn>157.8</mn></math>. It&rsquo;s given that an area of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"46.00\"><mn>46.00</mn></math> square nautical miles is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math> square kilometers. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math> nautical mile is equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1.852\"><mn>1.852</mn></math> kilometers, it follows that an area of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"1\"><mn>1</mn></math> square nautical mile is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"left parenthesis 1.852 right parenthesis squared\"><msup><mrow><mo>(</mo><mn>1.852</mn><mo>)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3.429904\"><mn>3.429904</mn></math>, square kilometers. Multiplying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"46.00\"><mn>46.00</mn></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"3.429904\"><mn>3.429904</mn></math> yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"157.775584\"><mn>157.775584</mn></math> square kilometers. Rounding this value to the nearest tenth yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"157.8\"><mn>157.8</mn></math>. Therefore, the value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"k\"><mi>k</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" alttext=\"157.8\"><mn>157.8</mn></math>.</p>","externalid":"74b99987-9634-407d-adbe-a305f02a1c5f","correct_answer":["157.8","789/5"]},"createDate":1776362727992,"updateDate":1776362727992}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362727992, 1776362727992),
    ($SATQ$c78bb2b8$SATQ$, $SATQ$a97f29fd-deb9-4f76-baff-4f4bea70e929$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$Math$SATQ$, $SATQ$Problem-Solving and Data Analysis$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q$SATQ$, $SATQ$Q.C.$SATQ$, $SATQ$One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, NULL, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">A scientist measured the lengths of <math alttext="240"><mn>240</mn>
</math> gray seals from Muskeget Island and <math alttext="120"><mn>120</mn>
</math> gray seals from Sable Island. The scientist determined that the mean length of the <math alttext="240"><mn>240</mn>
</math> gray seals from Muskeget Island was <math alttext="88"><mn>88</mn>
</math> inches and the mean length of the <math alttext="120"><mn>120</mn>
</math> gray seals from Sable Island was <math alttext="94"><mn>94</mn>
</math> inches. What was the mean length of all <math alttext="360"><mn>360</mn>
</math> gray seals the scientist measured for this study?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"f5ba2b41-529b-4d00-8022-da926b370d54","content":"<p><math alttext=\"89\"><mn>89</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9bec182e-d082-4489-95d6-bc841ae5bb45","content":"<p><math alttext=\"90\"><mn>90</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d238b969-3d69-4f8b-88c7-9932195f9695","content":"<p><math alttext=\"91\"><mn>91</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1b2801fd-5a64-445f-869d-df184b7deef9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"92\"><mn>92</mn>\n</math></p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9bec182e-d082-4489-95d6-bc841ae5bb45"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p style="text-align: left;">Choice B is correct. The mean of a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of the values in the data set by the number of values in the data set. It&rsquo;s given that the mean length of the <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="240"><mn>240</mn></math> gray seals from Muskeget Island was <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="88"><mn>88</mn></math> inches. This can be represented by the equation <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction x Over 240 EndFraction equals 88"><mfrac><mi>x</mi><mn>240</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>88</mn></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x"><mi>x</mi></math> represents the sum of the lengths, in inches, of the <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="240"><mn>240</mn></math> gray seals from Muskeget Island. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="240"><mn>240</mn></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals left parenthesis 240 right parenthesis left parenthesis 88 right parenthesis"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>240</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>88</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="x equals 21,120"><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>21,120</mn></math> inches. It&rsquo;s also given that the mean length of the <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="120"><mn>120</mn></math> gray seals from Sable Island was <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="94"><mn>94</mn></math> inches. This can be represented by the equation <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction y Over 120 EndFraction equals 94"><mfrac><mi>y</mi><mn>120</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>94</mn></math>, where <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y"><mi>y</mi></math> represents the sum of the lengths, in inches, of the <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="120"><mn>120</mn></math> gray seals from Sable Island. Multiplying both sides of this equation by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="120"><mn>120</mn></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y equals left parenthesis 120 right parenthesis left parenthesis 94 right parenthesis"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>120</mn><mo>)</mo><mo>(</mo><mn>94</mn><mo>)</mo></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="y equals 11,280"><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>11,280</mn></math> inches. Therefore, the sum of the lengths, in inches, of all <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="360"><mn>360</mn></math> gray seals is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="21,120 plus 11,280"><mn>21,120</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>11,280</mn></math>, or <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="32,400"><mn>32,400</mn></math>. Dividing this sum by <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="360"><mn>360</mn></math> yields <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="StartFraction 32,400 Over 360 EndFraction"><mfrac><mn>32,400</mn><mn>360</mn></mfrac></math>, or&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="90"><mn>90</mn></math> inches. Therefore, the mean length of all <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="360"><mn>360</mn></math> gray seals the scientist measured for this study is <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="90"><mn>90</mn></math> inches.</p>
<p>Choice A is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>
<p>Choice C is incorrect. This is the mean length of <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="88"><mn>88</mn></math> inches and <math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="94"><mn>94</mn></math> inches, not the mean length of all&nbsp;<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="360"><mn>360</mn></math> gray seals the scientist measured for this study.</p>
<p>Choice D is incorrect and may result from conceptual or calculation errors.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"c78bb2b8","external_id":"a97f29fd-deb9-4f76-baff-4f4bea70e929","disclosed_item_id":null,"source":"qbank","vaultid":null,"uId":"e1c55017-182f-4520-b0ff-7865b05c7daa","program":"SAT","test_type":2,"test_type_desc":"Math","primary_class_cd":"Q","domain":"Problem-Solving and Data Analysis","skill_cd":"Q.C.","skill_desc":"One-variable data: Distributions and measures of center and spread","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":null,"stem":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">A scientist measured the lengths of <math alttext=\"240\"><mn>240</mn>\n</math> gray seals from Muskeget Island and <math alttext=\"120\"><mn>120</mn>\n</math> gray seals from Sable Island. The scientist determined that the mean length of the <math alttext=\"240\"><mn>240</mn>\n</math> gray seals from Muskeget Island was <math alttext=\"88\"><mn>88</mn>\n</math> inches and the mean length of the <math alttext=\"120\"><mn>120</mn>\n</math> gray seals from Sable Island was <math alttext=\"94\"><mn>94</mn>\n</math> inches. What was the mean length of all <math alttext=\"360\"><mn>360</mn>\n</math> gray seals the scientist measured for this study?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"f5ba2b41-529b-4d00-8022-da926b370d54","content":"<p><math alttext=\"89\"><mn>89</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"9bec182e-d082-4489-95d6-bc841ae5bb45","content":"<p><math alttext=\"90\"><mn>90</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"d238b969-3d69-4f8b-88c7-9932195f9695","content":"<p><math alttext=\"91\"><mn>91</mn>\n</math></p>"},{"id":"1b2801fd-5a64-445f-869d-df184b7deef9","content":"<p><math alttext=\"92\"><mn>92</mn>\n</math></p>"}],"keys":["9bec182e-d082-4489-95d6-bc841ae5bb45"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p style=\"text-align: left;\">Choice B is correct. The mean of a data set is calculated by dividing the sum of the values in the data set by the nu